{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"psychotic-selfless-ragfish","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Problem\n\nOn an arm64 macOS host, 17 tests fail because the `pypandoc` binary bundled by the `wiki` extra is **Mach-O x86_64**. Executing it raises `Errno 86, Bad CPU type in executable`. No system pandoc is present to fall back to.\n\nThis is **not a sandbox problem.** It breaks `make verify` for every Apple Silicon developer, sandboxed or not. It was found during the sandbox census under `985f-0ec5-a61c-46ee` and split out here because it has a different cause and the opposite remedy.\n\n## Why skipping is the wrong fix, and is ruled out\n\nThe obvious remedy — probe for a working pandoc and skip when absent — **contradicts a deliberate, documented invariant.** `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_wiki_render.py:6-9` states it plainly:\n\n> These tests NEVER `importorskip` and never monkeypatch pandoc. The `wiki` extra is installed in dev/CI, and the pin assertions below are the point — a silently absent or drifted pandoc must FAIL, not skip.\n\nADR 0095 backs this: the pin is gated on Linux and macOS CI, and the `wiki` extra is **required** in the test environment. Those tests exist precisely to fail when pandoc is absent or drifted. Making them skip would delete the signal they were written to produce.\n\nThe failing tests must therefore be made to **pass**, by providing a pandoc that runs on the host. This ticket does not make the gate quieter; it makes the dependency correct.\n\n## Two distinct test classes\n\nA remedy must not treat these alike:\n\n| Module | Gate | Required behaviour when pandoc is unusable |\n|---|---|---|\n| `test_wiki_render.py` | **None** — runs unconditionally, calls `wiki_render._pandoc_path()` then `subprocess.run` directly | Must **FAIL**. This is the documented contract. |\n| `test_wiki_render_hardening.py` | `_NEEDS_PANDOC = skipif(_pandoc_path() is None)` at line 56 | May skip, and already does |\n\nA change that flips the second class without fixing the first leaves the failures in place. A change that makes the first class skip breaks the invariant.\n\n## Correcting a false premise\n\nThe `985f` census described this as a `shutil.which` presence check that reports a binary which cannot run. That is wrong and should not be carried forward. `wiki_render._pandoc_path()` (`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_family/wiki_render.py:134-147`) resolves through `pypandoc.get_pandoc_path()`, guarded for `ImportError` and `OSError`. No `shutil.which` pandoc check exists in that path. The `shutil.which` gates that do exist are in the grounding tests and concern semgrep, not pandoc.\n\nThere are already two detectors — `wiki_render._pandoc_path()` and `rebar._capabilities.is_available('datacenter_wiki')`, the latter using `find_spec` and so structurally unable to detect an unrunnable binary. **Do not add a third.** Any probe change extends an existing seam.\n\n## Approach\n\nEstablish which of these holds, then act on it — this is an investigation before a fix, and the answer determines the remedy:\n\n1. Whether the pinned `pypandoc` distribution publishes an arm64 wheel or an arm64-capable binary at the pinned version. If it does, the fix is a packaging correction.\n2. Whether the project should depend on a system pandoc on arm64 instead of the bundled binary, with the pin assertions verifying the resolved binary's version either way.\n3. Whether the pin itself must move to a version with arm64 support, which brings ADR 0095's pinning rationale into scope.\n\nWhichever holds, `_pandoc_path()` must resolve to a binary that **executes on the host architecture**, so that a present-but-unrunnable binary is never reported as a usable pandoc.\n\n## Testing strategy\n\nTDD: the failing test comes first.\n\n- A test asserts that the pandoc resolved by `_pandoc_path()` executes successfully on the host and reports a version, so a binary of the wrong architecture fails the assertion rather than surfacing as an opaque `Errno 86` deep in a subprocess call.\n- The existing pin assertions in `test_wiki_render.py` remain unconditional. They are the regression oracle and must not gain a skip gate.\n- Regression evidence: on an arm64 host, the 17 failures reach 0 with no test skipped.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Running `make test` on an arm64 macOS host reports 0 failures in the 17-test pandoc population. Verified by running `make test` on such a host and counting failures in `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/`.\n- [ ] None of the 17 tests is skipped on an arm64 host. Verified by asserting the skip count for `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/` is unchanged from an x86_64 baseline run.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_wiki_render.py` retains its unconditional execution with no `importorskip` and no `skipif`. Verified by `grep -c \"importorskip\\|skipif\" tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_wiki_render.py` returning 0.\n- [ ] A test asserts that `wiki_render._pandoc_path()` returns a binary that executes and reports a version on the host. Verified by that test failing when pointed at a stub binary that exits with `Errno 86`.\n- [ ] No third pandoc detector is introduced. Verified by confirming the count of pandoc-resolution call sites is unchanged from `origin/main`.\n- [ ] `make test` on x86_64 CI executes the same test count as on `origin/main` today. Verified by comparing collected counts in the CI log.\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` reports 0 new unadmitted mechanisms. Verified by running that command.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nThe git-identity, semgrep and `ps` sandbox causes (`985f-0ec5-a61c-46ee`). The SemLock denials (`0eba-cfd8-34b0-4cb7`).\n\n## Escalation clause\n\nIf no option provides an arm64-capable pandoc at a version compatible with the ADR 0095 pin, do not resolve it by weakening the pin assertions or by adding a skip to `test_wiki_render.py`. State the constraint and the real options, including the cost of each. \"Apple Silicon cannot run this gate without changing the pin\" is a valuable finding and is preferable to a remedy that silently removes the drift signal.","id":"dc28-2807-5b1d-4da7","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Bundled pypandoc binary is x86_64-only, so 17 wiki-render tests fail on every arm64 host"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1789693124094850381,"uuid":"b9197e0e-8e50-4a3d-b97a-be4c0862956e"}